          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crossed+complex"} Crossed complexes
            are a useful extension of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crossed+module"} crossed module
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+2-type"} homotopy 2-type
            = succeed "\u2019." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                complex of chains on the universal cover
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/complex+of+chains+on+the+universal+cover"} ?
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+closed+category"} monoidal closed category
            equivalent to various types of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+infinity-groupoid"} strict infinity-groupoid
          %p
            To model the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+3-type"} homotopy 3-type
            of a space, we can use either a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-crossed+module"} 2-crossed module
            or a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crossed+square"} crossed square
            (or various other algebraic models to be added some time in the future). A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crossed+complex"} crossed complex
            is a ‘hybrid’, part
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crossed+module"} crossed module
            but with a ‘tail’ which is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complex
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A
            %strong 2-crossed complex
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+complex+of+groups"} normal complex of groups
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi …
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi n
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi …
                  %mo ⟶
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ldots \to C_n \stackrel{\partial_n}{\longrightarrow} C_{n-1} \longrightarrow \ldots \longrightarrow C_0,
          %p
            together with a 2-crossed module structure given on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 2
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_2\to C_1\to C_0
            by a Peiffer lifting function
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ×
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ -,-\} : C_1\times C_1 \to C_2
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                    %mo =
                    %mi Coker
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi = Coker(C_1\to C_0)
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                each
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_n
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 3
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\geq 3
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Ker
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mo ∂
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ker \,\partial_2
                are
                = succeed "-modules" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi π
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial_n
                for
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo ≥
                        %mn 4
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\geq 4
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ∂
                          %mn 3
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial_3
                = succeed "-module" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi π
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
            %li
              %p
                the
                = succeed "-module" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi π
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Ker
                      %msub
                        %mo ∂
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ker \partial_2
                is the action induced from the
                = succeed "-action" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_0
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_2
                for which the action of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∂
                        %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial_1 C_1
                is trivial.
          %p A 2-crossed complex morphism is defined in the obvious way, being compatible with all the actions, the pairings and Peiffer liftings.
          %p
            We will denote by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %mi Crs
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2 Crs
          %h2#examples Examples:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Any
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-crossed+module"} 2-crossed module
                clearly gives a 2-crossed complex (with trivial ‘tail’).
            %li
              %p
                %em> From simplicial groups to 2-crossed complexes
                \. If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                is a simplicial group, then
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi …
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mn 3
                  %mo →
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒩
                      %msub
                        %mi G
                        %mn 2
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒩
                      %msub
                        %mi G
                        %mn 3
                      %mo ∩
                      %msub
                        %mi D
                        %mn 3
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒩
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒩
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ldots \to C(G)_3 \to  \frac{\mathcal{N}G_2}{d_0(\mathcal{N}G_3\cap D_3)} \to \mathcal{N}G_1\to \mathcal{N}G_0,
          %p
            has the structure of a 2-crossed complex, where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒩
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{N}G
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moore+complex"} Moore complex
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_n
            is the subgroup of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_n
            generated by the degenerate elements, and, for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\gt2
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒩
                      %msub
                        %mi G
                        %mi n
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒩
                      %msub
                        %mi G
                        %mi n
                      %mo ∩
                      %msub
                        %mi D
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒩
                      %msub
                        %mi G
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                      %mo ∩
                      %msub
                        %mi D
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {C}(G)_{n} = \frac{\mathcal{N}G_n}{(\mathcal{N}G_n\cap D_n)d_0(\mathcal{N}G_{n+1}\cap D_{n+1})},
          %p
            is the
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(G)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            as in the entry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crossed+complex"} crossed complex
            (in the section
            = succeed ".)" do
              %strong From simplicial group(oid)s to crossed complexes
          %p (There is an obvious extension of the group based definition above to a groupoid based one, and of this construction to one which takes as input a simplicially enriched groupoid.)
          %p
            The Moore complex of a simplicial group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            has the structure of a 2-crossed complex if and only if for each
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\gt 2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒩
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mi n
                  %mo ∩
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{N}G_n\cap D_n
            is trivial. This means that the axioms of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+T-complex"} group T-complex
            are almost satisfied, but not necessarily in dimension 2.
          %ul
            %li
              %p A quadratic chain complex as defined by H.J. Baues is a special case of a 2-crossed complex, satisfying additional (pre-crossed module) nilpotency condition at the level of the underlying pre-crossed module. (In fact the category of quadratic chain complexes is a reflective subcategory of the category of 2-crossed complexes.) In Baues’ book referenced below, there is the construction of the fundamental quadratic chain complex of a pointed CW-complex. The reflection (or cotruncation) of this to the category of quadratic modules (i.e. 3-truncated quadratic chain complexes) faithfully represents the homotopy 3-type of a CW-space (at the level of spaces and maps between them).
            %li
              %p Graham Ellis defined the fundamental squared complex of a CW-complex from triad homotopy groups and generalised Whitehead products, and showed how Baues fundamental quadratic chain complex of a CW-complex can be obtained from it. A homotopy 2-crossed complex of a CW-complex can also be defined is the same way, see the work of João Faria Martins below.
          %h2#crossed_complexes_and_2crossed_complexes Crossed complexes and 2-crossed complexes.
          %p
            Any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crossed+complex"} crossed complex
            can be given the structure of a 2-crossed complex simply by defining a trivial Peiffer lifting,
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{-,-\}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_1
          %p
            Any 2-crossed complex which has a Peiffer lifting that is trivial
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x,y\} = 1
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x,y \in C_1
          %p
            This functor, from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Crs
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Crs
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %mo −
                    %mi Crs
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2-Crs
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %mo −
                  %mi Crs
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2-Crs
            with trivial Peiffer liftings, so
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Crs
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Crs
            is equivalent to a reflective subcategory of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %mo −
                  %mi Crs
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2-Crs
